1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,832
A metasurface is a flat sheet, patterned at a scale smaller than the
wavelength of light.

2
00:00:06,152 --> 00:00:14,936
Each of those tiny structures delays the wave as it passes, so a sheet
thinner than a human hair can focus, steer, or colour light.

3
00:00:15,256 --> 00:00:20,968
Flat lenses, holograms, colour printing and beam steering all rely on them.

4
00:00:21,288 --> 00:00:29,592
Designing one means choosing its geometry cell by cell: sixty four by sixty
four, each cell empty or filled.

5
00:00:29,912 --> 00:00:34,880
Here is the response we want: how the wave comes back, at every frequency.

6
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:43,648
The physics only runs the other way. From a structure we can compute the
response; from the response, the structure is not unique.

7
00:00:43,968 --> 00:00:49,128
Can a machine learn to design a structure from the electromagnetic behaviour
we ask for?

8
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:59,124
In the forward direction the physics is well posed: give it a geometry and
three global parameters, and it returns a spectrum.

9
00:00:59,444 --> 00:01:05,996
The three parameters are the lattice period l, the meta-atom height h, and
the refractive index r.

10
00:01:06,316 --> 00:01:13,348
Inverse design reverses that arrow. We are handed the spectrum and must
infer geometry and parameters.

11
00:01:13,668 --> 00:01:21,996
The map is many to one. These two designs, from the released test set,
produce spectra that differ by about one percent.

12
00:01:22,316 --> 00:01:28,508
So the model must use the target response, the visible structure, and
whichever parameters it knows.

13
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:37,804
The data is the released MetaDiT corpus: one hundred seventy four thousand,
eight hundred eighty three samples.

14
00:01:38,124 --> 00:01:47,220
Each sample pairs a sixty four by sixty four occupancy map with three global
scalars and a two by three hundred one complex spectrum.

15
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:52,556
The release packs that into three image channels; the model factors it
apart.

16
00:01:52,876 --> 00:02:02,044
Geometry becomes one binary map, the scalars become three numbers with known
flags, and the two uncertainties become separable.

17
00:02:03,633 --> 00:02:10,689
Rather than predicting missing pixels directly, the model predicts the
representation of the missing information.

18
00:02:11,009 --> 00:02:17,561
Cut the geometry into a sixteen by sixteen grid of patches. Each patch
becomes a token.

19
00:02:17,881 --> 00:02:25,633
Hide some of them. A student network sees only what is visible, plus the
target spectrum, plus whatever scalars are known.

20
00:02:25,953 --> 00:02:33,177
A teacher sees the complete geometry and the true parameters, and produces a
latent representation of the answer.

21
00:02:33,497 --> 00:02:38,201
The student is asked to match that representation at the hidden positions.

22
00:02:38,521 --> 00:02:47,665
That is the JEPA loss: the squared distance in latent space, with variance
and covariance terms that keep the representation from collapsing.

23
00:02:47,985 --> 00:02:53,505
Only afterwards does a decoder convert those latents back into physical
geometry.

24
00:02:55,100 --> 00:03:00,692
Here is the complete network. One width throughout: one hundred ninety two
dimensions.

25
00:03:01,012 --> 00:03:10,516
The occupancy encoder is a four by four patch embedding with stride four:
sixty four squared pixels become two hundred fifty six tokens.

26
00:03:10,836 --> 00:03:15,444
Six transformer blocks follow, each modulated by the scalars.

27
00:03:15,764 --> 00:03:24,284
The scalar encoder is small: six inputs, a value and a known flag each,
through a one hundred twenty eight wide trunk.

28
00:03:24,604 --> 00:03:28,540
It emits six modulation pairs, and one summary token.

29
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:35,292
The modulation is FiLM: the feature is scaled and shifted, gamma times the
feature plus beta.

30
00:03:35,612 --> 00:03:44,228
The target spectrum passes through the released encoder, frozen and never
trained, giving local features at all three hundred one points.

31
00:03:44,548 --> 00:03:52,588
Two readouts: a mean-pooled global condition of three hundred eighty four
dimensions, and sixteen learned goal tokens.

32
00:03:52,908 --> 00:04:03,924
Fusion concatenates two hundred fifty six occupancy tokens, sixteen goal
tokens projected to one hundred ninety two, and the scalar token: two
hundred seventy three.

33
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:12,020
And now the spectrum is in the same space as the geometry: sixteen goal
tokens, and a global physics condition.

34
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:19,804
The predictor holds two hundred fifty seven queries: one for every occupancy
position, and one scalar query.

35
00:04:20,124 --> 00:04:32,628
Each of its eight blocks does three things: the queries attend to each
other, then read all two hundred seventy three context tokens — spectral
goals included — then pass through an MLP.

36
00:04:32,948 --> 00:04:38,108
The result is two hundred fifty six occupancy latents and one scalar latent.

37
00:04:39,633 --> 00:04:44,793
The teacher is not trained by backpropagation. It is a moving average of the
student.

38
00:04:45,113 --> 00:04:54,401
After every optimiser step the teacher blends in the student, with tau
rising from zero point nine nine six to zero point nine nine nine.

39
00:04:54,721 --> 00:04:59,857
That slow copy is a stable target, and no gradient crosses the boundary.

40
00:05:00,177 --> 00:05:07,569
Three targets come out of it: a stable geometry target, a
spectrum-conditioned geometry target, and a scalar target.

41
00:05:07,889 --> 00:05:14,993
The objective has three groups. First, latent learning: invariance,
variance, covariance.

42
00:05:15,313 --> 00:05:23,689
Second, direct supervision: occupancy cross-entropy, scalar error on the
unknowns, and two cosine latent targets.

43
00:05:24,009 --> 00:05:29,385
Third, the physics term — a hybrid objective, not pure self-supervision.

44
00:05:30,967 --> 00:05:32,839
The latents become a design.

45
00:05:33,159 --> 00:05:46,215
The occupancy decoder reshapes the two hundred fifty six tokens into a
sixteen by sixteen grid, then upsamples to sixty four by sixty four. It
predicts occupancy only, one channel.

46
00:05:46,535 --> 00:05:55,103
The scalar decoder predicts the three parameters. Known parameters keep
their supplied values; unknown ones use the prediction.

47
00:05:55,423 --> 00:06:05,455
Only here, at the surrogate boundary, are the three geometry channels
assembled: refractive index over five, height, and lattice period over
three.

48
00:06:05,775 --> 00:06:10,959
A frozen electromagnetic surrogate maps that geometry to a spectrum.

49
00:06:11,279 --> 00:06:18,263
The weights are frozen, but the gradient flows back through the input, so
the physics error trains the decoder.

50
00:06:18,583 --> 00:06:23,287
Full-wave simulation is a separate evaluation tool, not part of training.

51
00:06:24,867 --> 00:06:35,787
Training varies the problem: occupancy masked at twenty five, fifty, seventy
five or one hundred percent, and scalars all known, mixed, or unknown.

52
00:06:36,107 --> 00:06:48,035
Each step samples data, draws a mask, drops scalars, runs student and
teacher, totals the losses, backpropagates, updates the student, then moves
the teacher.

53
00:06:48,355 --> 00:06:55,291
Physics ramps in from step two thousand to five thousand; the multi-target
terms from step one thousand.

54
00:06:55,611 --> 00:07:05,403
At inference the teacher and every loss are gone. The student and the frozen
spectrum path remain, and the surrogate is only needed to score the result.

55
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,048
One model, three different conditioning problems.

56
00:07:10,368 --> 00:07:20,400
Scenario A is pure inverse design. Every occupancy token is hidden and all
three scalars are unknown, so only the target spectrum is given.

57
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:30,464
Scenario B hides half the geometry and gives one known scalar. The model
combines partial structure, one parameter, and the target response.

58
00:07:30,784 --> 00:07:40,600
Scenario C is a retrofit: a quarter of the geometry is hidden, the
parameters are known, and the visible pixels must survive untouched.

59
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:45,792
Measured on that property, the visible region changes by exactly zero.

60
00:07:47,333 --> 00:07:58,925
The model is small: eleven point four million trainable parameters.
MetaDiT-S carries thirty two point six million — nearly three times as many
— for higher error.

61
00:07:59,245 --> 00:08:03,277
One forward pass, against five hundred denoising steps.

62
00:08:03,597 --> 00:08:14,733
Three training seeds give a test MAE of zero point zero four nine zero, plus
or minus zero point zero zero one three, and an AAE of twenty nine point
five.

63
00:08:15,053 --> 00:08:28,589
The gates ask: is the error with the correct target lower than with a
shuffled one? The win fractions are zero point nine nine six seven, zero
point nine seven seven two, and zero point nine three zero three.

64
00:08:28,909 --> 00:08:39,661
The controlled ablation changes only the objective. Without the JEPA latent
machinery, MAE rises to zero point zero nine two two, nearly twice as large.

65
00:08:39,981 --> 00:08:51,693
Against our own reproduction of MetaDiT-S, same split and same surrogate,
the unified model reaches zero point zero four nine zero against zero point
zero eight zero three.

66
00:08:53,300 --> 00:09:01,292
Why does this work? Because the spectrum is many to one: it fixes the
response, not every pixel of the structure.

67
00:09:01,612 --> 00:09:07,876
A generator that draws pixels spends capacity on detail the physics does not
fix.

68
00:09:08,196 --> 00:09:16,548
JEPA instead predicts a representation under the physics condition, and a
variance term forbids the trivial answer of predicting nothing.

69
00:09:16,868 --> 00:09:26,132
The hardest stratum shows why: with everything masked, the true spectrum
beats a shuffled one on ninety nine point seven percent of items.

70
00:09:26,452 --> 00:09:32,284
So one small network, in a single pass, turns a desired response into a
design.

71
00:09:32,604 --> 00:09:44,004
The same argument travels to inverse design in other fields, to scientific
surrogates, and to world models — predict the state that matters, not every
pixel of the future.

72
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:48,720
None of this is a diagram. These are the files.

73
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:56,120
The occupancy encoder is one convolution, then six blocks, each followed by
the scalar modulation.

74
00:09:56,440 --> 00:10:04,456
The forward pass builds the two token sets: two hundred seventy three
context tokens, two hundred fifty seven queries.

75
00:10:04,776 --> 00:10:13,344
Every GCLCT block is these lines: normalise, condition, self-attend,
cross-attend, then an MLP.

76
00:10:13,664 --> 00:10:19,664
And the physics loss decodes, assembles the three channels, and calls the
frozen surrogate.

77
00:10:19,984 --> 00:10:22,984
Every number in this film comes from these files.

78
00:10:24,599 --> 00:10:31,463
One model, one width, three input streams, and a target that lives in
representation space.

79
00:10:31,783 --> 00:10:39,559
Eleven point four million parameters, one forward pass, and lower error than
a diffusion model three times its size.

80
00:10:39,879 --> 00:10:47,103
From a desired response, to a learned representation, to a candidate
geometry, and back through physics.

81
00:10:47,423 --> 00:10:50,783
Learning the representations behind physical design.

